TY  - JOUR
ID  - EX01
TI  - Supervised motor control exercise versus usual care for chronic low back pain: a randomized controlled trial
AU  - Example, Ann
AU  - Sample, Ben
PY  - 2021
T2  - Journal of Synthetic Examples
DO  - 10.5555/lls-example.0001
AB  - Background: Supervised exercise is widely recommended for chronic low back pain, but its effect compared with usual care is uncertain. Methods: In this randomized controlled trial, 160 adults with non-specific low back pain lasting more than 12 weeks were allocated to 12 weeks of supervised motor control exercise or usual care from their general practitioner. The primary outcomes were pain intensity and back-specific disability at 3 and 12 months. Results: Supervised exercise reduced pain intensity and disability compared with usual care at 3 months; the difference in disability persisted at 12 months. Conclusions: Supervised motor control exercise is a useful treatment for adults with chronic low back pain.
N1  - SYNTHETIC RECORD: written for the llm-literature-screener example. It does not describe a real study.
